    "https://hartford.peopleadmin.com/postings/8062": {
      "title": "Clinical/Applied Assistant Professor of Psychology (2027 2028)",
      "description": "University of Hartford Employment Site | Clinical/Applied Assistant Professor of Psychology (2027 2028) Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Clinical/Applied Assistant Professor of Psychology (2027 2028) Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Job Title Clinical/Applied Assistant Professor of Psychology (2027 2028) Rank Assistant Professor Salary Range $68,000 Tenure Information Clinical/Applied Job Description The Hillyer College of Arts and Sciences at the University of Hartford is seeking to fill a clinical/applied assistant professor position in Cognitive or Biological Psychology beginning August 2026. Candidates are expected to teach and advise students in our undergraduate psychology program. Responsibilities The successful applicant will ordinarily teach courses in statistics and research methods, as well as in their area of expertise with a preference for Cognitive or Biological Psychology or closely related area. There may also be opportunities to teach our innovative general education and interdisciplinary courses. The ideal candidate will demonstrate professional productivity as evidenced by sustained excellence and innovation in their clinical/applied practice, contributions to the profession through publications, presentations, or other publicly disseminated work, as well as service to academic, professional, or Hartford area communities. Service to the department, college, and university typically includes advising students, contributing to student recruitment and retention, and participating in departmental and college committees. Candidates are expected to demonstrate a commitment to inclusive excellence, which the University defines as an unwavering commitment to fostering a vibrant campus community where every student, staff member, and faculty colleague feels deeply rooted, empowered to flourish, and inspired to achieve their highest potential. The initial Clinical/Applied contract is 1-2 years and subsequent longer-term contracts are based on review of performance and the continuing need for the position. For best consideration, complete applications should be received by November 30. Please submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, teaching philosophy, statement on inclusive excellence and belonging, and the names of three references who will electronically submit letters of recommendation upon request at a later stage of the review process. Optional documents that may also be included are samples of teaching effectiveness and a research philosophy that addresses how undergraduates may be involved in the applicant’s research program or applied activities. Required Qualifications Required Candidates must have completed a doctorate in psychology or a related discipline by the time of appointment. Preferred Strong experience teaching undergraduates in psychology, statistics, or research methods. Area of expertise in cognitive or biological psychology. Commitment and ability to mentor and actively engage undergraduate students with a focus on inclusive excellence and belonging. Research or applied experience. Interpersonal, organizational, and communication skills, and ability to work with students, staff, and faculty. Preferred Qualifications Statement on Inclusive Excellence and Teaching Philosophy Statement of Inclusive Excellence As an institution of higher learning, the University of Hartford strives to be more than merely a mirror of the larger society; it should foster learning and encourage the personal growth of students, faculty, and staff in an environment that promotes and celebrates diversity in all of its forms regardless of identifying factors including but not limited to race, sex, age, religion, national origin, socioeconomic status, physical and learning differences, sexual orientation, gender or gender identity. Accordingly, our commitment is to: · Foster an inclusive, equitable, just, and caring community where the unique identities of each person are fully appreciated, respected, and celebrated · Engage in civil discourse, that values diverse intellectual perspectives · Cultivate a campus community reflective of the intersectionality of the larger society while maintaining an environment of safety and respect where all members experience a sense of belonging · Institutionally, stand against acts of hate, intimidation, discrimination, bigotry, violence, and harassment of any kind · Uphold the values of the institution, so that all our members are treated with dignity and respect Our commitment to Inclusive Excellence and Belonging is a strength that results in a vibrant and more engaged campus culture. Teaching Philosophy In the teaching philosophy statement, please share your thoughts and experiences in using active learning strategies, high-impact practices, and evidence-based pedagogical practices, as well as your methods for engaging students and fostering student belonging in the classroom. University Information Students at the University of Hartford find success in a learning environment that both challenges and mentors them. Our academic mission is to engage students in acquiring the knowledge, skills, and values necessary to thrive in, and contribute to, a pluralistic, complex world. At the University of Hartford, we are committed to community. We are an academic community that values integrity, curiosity, creativity, excellence, responsibility, and accomplishment. Enriched by our engagement with, and understanding of, one another, we take pride in our shared traditions and experiences. We are dedicated to building a culture that respects all of its members and celebrates their contributions as we work together to strengthen our community. The full text of our academic mission, vision, and values can be seen at https:// www.hartford.edu . Opportunities for faculty development and support can be found at https://www.hartford.edu/about/campus-leadership/office-provost/faculty-development/ and a listing of Affinity Networks can be found at https://www.hartford.edu/about/diversity-inclusion/affinity-groups.aspx College Information Hillyer College of Arts and Sciences is one of the University’s largest colleges, with 24 undergraduate majors, 27 minors, 5 graduate programs, and many interdisciplinary offerings. The College includes a faculty of roughly 100 educators and scholars and 18 staff, and it boasts a student body of 780 undergraduate and 280 graduate students. Posting Detail Information Posting Number F227P Open Date 08/16/2027 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Special Instructions to Applicants Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Are you currently or have you ever been a part-time faculty member at the University of Hartford? Yes No Documents Needed To Apply Required Documents Statement on Inclusive Excellence and Belonging Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching Philosophy Optional Documents Research Philosophy Samples of Teaching Effectiveness 200 Bloomfield Avenue\r\nWest Hartford, CT 06117 Back to top Copyright © 2021 University of Hartford To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://hartford.peopleadmin.com/postings/8049": {
      "title": "Student Success Coach and Academic Advisor",
      "description": "University of Hartford Employment Site | Student Success Coach and Academic Advisor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Student Success Coach and Academic Advisor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Student Success Coach and Academic Advisor FLSA Classification EX Reports to Associate Director of Student Success Salary Range G Weeks Per Year 52 Work Week 40 hours (minimum) Position Summary Assumes responsibility for promoting a positive and successful experience for first‑ and second-year students in support of student retention and graduation initiatives. Provides support for evidence‑based retention programming, advising, and first‑year experience curriculum. Contributes to department‑level committees focused on student success initiatives, academic advising systems, and data‑informed decision‑making. All duties are performed in full support of the University’s mission, with a strong commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion, recognizing that the effective execution of these responsibilities is central to student learning, persistence, and success. Hiring Range Starting salary is budgeted between $45,868.00 and $51,000, depending upon experience Key Responsibilities Key Responsibilities Student Success Initiatives Implements intentional, evidence‑based programs, policies, and procedures designed to support student success, retention, and persistence. Assists with short‑ and long‑range planning for student retention initiatives, with an emphasis on first‑year student transition and sustained success beyond the first year. Actively participates in departmental committees focused on student retention programs, advising technologies, data analysis, or other critical student success functions. Collaborates with internal and external partners to execute and continuously improve student success initiatives. Academic Advising and Coaching Advises and coaches a caseload of approximately 140-160 first‑ and second-year students through their transition to the University. Ensures second-year students successfully transition to a faculty advisor within their major or program. Supports and monitors students’ academic progress, personal and social adjustment, major requirements, and success strategies. Advises students on course selection and assists with course registration processes. Proactively identifies and supports at‑risk students, connecting them with appropriate University resources and offices to address academic, personal, and social challenges. Empowers students to develop self‑advocacy skills and build a foundation for long‑term academic and personal success. Early Alert Systems and Data Integrity Utilizes the full functionality of the early alert and advising systems to document student appointments, degree progression, academic alerts, progress surveys, and interventions. Maintains accurate and timely student records while adhering to data integrity and confidentiality standards. Uses student data to inform advising strategies, outreach efforts, and program improvements. First ‑ Year Experience Curriculum and Instruction Delivers first‑year experience course curriculum, including learning outcomes, mandatory classroom activities, and assessment strategies. Instructs 4–6 sections of the First‑Year Experience course each academic year. Supports students’ holistic development by promoting campus engagement, resource awareness, and academic skill development. Serves as a resource to students and advisors both inside and outside the classroom, providing accurate and timely information and feedback. University Programs and Enrollment Support Actively participates in enrollment events and programs, orientation activities, and fall opening initiatives. Develops fall course schedule for new students based on degree requirements, student needs and accommodations, and student interests; continually adjusts schedules through the summer and academic year as needed and according to institutional regulations. Connects with students and families in advance of the semester to address questions related to course schedules, locations, textbooks, or other transitional needs. Collaboration and Service Delivery Establishes, cultivates, and maintains effective working relationships across the University of Hartford to support a positive and coordinated academic experience. Supports the University’s high-touch service model by responding to student and parent inquiries, processing paperwork and registrations, and serving as a liaison to other campus departments. May serve on University‑wide committees as assigned to advance institutional student success priorities. Perform other related duties as assigned in support of the Center for Student Success and University goals. Posting Detail Information Posting Number PS1263P Working Conditions Normal office situtation. Education Bachelor’s Degree Required Physical Effort Typically sitting at a desk or table. Light lifting or carrying, 25lbs or less. Special Skills The ability to work effectively with diverse groups. Desired Start Date Position End Date (if temporary) Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Special Instructions Summary Quick Link for Internal Postings https://hartford.peopleadmin.com/postings/8048 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). What is the highest level of education attained? GED High School Diploma Technical School, Certification or Apprenticeship Associates Degree Bachelors Degree Masters Degree Knowledge of a highly advanced professional discipline. (e.g., Ph.D, M.D., J.D., or equivalent doctoral degree required.) How did you hear about this employment opportunity? Public Job Posting Internal Job Posting Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Website Other Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Optional Documents Resume Cover Letter Letter of Recommendation - Staff Statement on Inclusive Excellence and Belonging 200 Bloomfield Avenue\r\nWest Hartford, CT 06117 Back to top Copyright © 2021 University of Hartford To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://hartford.peopleadmin.com/postings/8063": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Associate Professor of Dance and Division Director of Dance (2027 2028)",
      "description": "University of Hartford Employment Site | Tenure Track Associate Professor of Dance and Division Director of Dance (2027 2028) Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Tenure Track Associate Professor of Dance and Division Director of Dance (2027 2028) Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Within the required Statement on Inclusive Excellence and Belonging, candidates should include an explanation of how their teaching practice meets the needs of the diverse population of students at the University of Hartford, focusing particularly on pedagogical approaches that support student success. If relevant, candidates should also comment on research, scholarship, practice, or creative activity that will contribute to the inclusive excellence goals to which the University of Hartford is committed. It is recommended that candidates approach this statement thoughtfully and use specific examples to illustrate their Statement on Inclusive Excellence and Belonging. In your cover letter, please relate your experience to all Qualifications of the position in addition to highlighting what unique skills you would bring to The Hartt School. Priority will be given to applications received by December 18, 2026; however, the search will remain open until the position is filled. Posting Details Position Information Job Title Tenure Track Associate Professor of Dance and Division Director of Dance (2027 2028) Rank Associate Professor Salary Range $77,000 - $90,000 Tenure Information Tenure Track Job Description The Hartt School, a conservatory of music, dance, and theatre, at the University of Hartford is seeking a tenure-track, Associate Professor in Dance beginning August 2027. Candidates are expected to teach dance to undergraduate students in the Dance Performance, Bachelor of Fine Arts program and to serve as Director of the Dance Division. This position reports to The Hartt School Dean. Responsibilities Manage all aspects of a complex dance division, including curricular and enrollment management; student recruitment; recommending part-time faculty hiring; recommending faculty loads and teaching assignments; completing faculty evaluations; scheduling and chairing division-wide meetings; addressing student and faculty issues; implementing all School and University policies; attending Dean’s Leadership Team, faculty, division, and committee meetings in person; providing input, advising on the artistic and academic direction for The Hartt School; and assisting in budgetary preparation and oversight. Teach dance to undergraduate students in the Dance Performance, Bachelor of Fine Arts program. Provide inclusive leadership with the faculty to remain current in dance curricula preparing students for long-term professional careers. Oversee and participate in all aspects of student recruitment, retention, and advising for enrollment and maintain a strong, inclusive, and diverse student body from the submission of the student’s application through their timely graduation. Meet with student groups and class representatives. Participate in the award of division scholarship funds in consultation with the Dean, Hartt Admission staff, and the dance faculty. Remain active in creative/scholarly pursuits, developing and/or maintaining a national and international profile in the field of dance and seek peer reviews, as appropriate—all as expected for the successful pursuit of tenure and promotion. Serve as a cooperative member of the Dean’s Leadership Team to support all aspects of The Hartt School, while serving as an advocate, ambassador, and spokesperson for the Dance Division to Hartt colleagues, University administration, the public at large, and the Hartford arts community. Serve as the institutional representative to the National Association of Schools of Dance ( NASD ). Serve as an advocate in fundraising for The Hartt School. Support the policies and regulatory processes of the University of Hartford and The Hartt School, ensuring equity, consistency, and effective communication, and precise recordkeeping. Serve on University and School committees as needed. Demonstrate a commitment to inclusive excellence, which the University defines as an unwavering commitment to fostering a vibrant campus community where every student, staff member, and faculty colleague feels deeply rooted, empowered to flourish, and inspired to achieve their highest potential. Required Qualifications Required Qualifications: Master of Fine Arts ( MFA ), or terminal degree, in a dance discipline at the time of appointment. A bachelor’s degree in dance with significant national, professional performing credits and experience will be considered. Ongoing record of professional experience, artistic achievement, and creative activities in dance. Evidence of successful university and/or conservatory teaching in a dance department with evidence of successful student mentorship experience. Evidence of successful leadership, organizational, and communication skills at a university and/or professional level and of the ability to collaborate effectively with a wide and culturally diverse range of students, faculty, staff, and administration. Preferred Qualifications: Evidence of curriculum development and innovative pedagogical practices or publications that offer a strong vision for dance training for the 21st century. Current working knowledge of performing arts high school networks and/or feeder systems. Experience working with the National Association of Schools of Dance accreditation process. Interest in, or experience with, community education and outreach programs. Commitment to Inclusive Excellence and Belonging: Candidate shows evidence of fostering an inclusive, equitable, just, and caring community where the unique identities of each person are fully appreciated, respected, and celebrated. Candidate demonstrates values that align with diverse intellectual perspectives. Candidate is capable of cultivating a campus community of respect and belonging. Preferred Qualifications Statement on Inclusive Excellence and Teaching Philosophy Statement of Inclusive Excellence As an institution of higher learning, the University of Hartford strives to be more than merely a mirror of the larger society; it should foster learning and encourage the personal growth of students, faculty, and staff in an environment that promotes and celebrates diversity in all of its forms regardless of identifying factors including but not limited to race, sex, age, religion, national origin, socioeconomic status, physical and learning differences, sexual orientation, gender or gender identity. Accordingly, our commitment is to: · Foster an inclusive, equitable, just, and caring community where the unique identities of each person are fully appreciated, respected, and celebrated · Engage in civil discourse, that values diverse intellectual perspectives · Cultivate a campus community reflective of the intersectionality of the larger society while maintaining an environment of safety and respect where all members experience a sense of belonging · Institutionally, stand against acts of hate, intimidation, discrimination, bigotry, violence, and harassment of any kind · Uphold the values of the institution, so that all our members are treated with dignity and respect Our commitment to Inclusive Excellence and Belonging is a strength that results in a vibrant and more engaged campus culture. Teaching Philosophy In the teaching philosophy statement, please share your thoughts and experiences in using active learning strategies, high-impact practices, and evidence-based pedagogical practices, as well as your methods for engaging students and fostering student belonging in the classroom. Please also include strategies for recruiting and retaining quality students and pedagogical methods for dance students. University Information Students at the University of Hartford find success in a learning environment that both challenges and mentors them. Our academic mission is to engage students in acquiring the knowledge, skills, and values necessary to thrive in, and contribute to, a pluralistic, complex world. At the University of Hartford, we are committed to community. We are an academic community that values integrity, curiosity, creativity, excellence, responsibility, and accomplishment. Enriched by our engagement with, and understanding of, one another, we take pride in our shared traditions and experiences. We are dedicated to building a culture that respects all of its members and celebrates their contributions as we work together to strengthen our community. The full text of our academic mission, vision, and values can be seen at https:// www.hartford.edu . Opportunities for faculty development and support can be found at https://www.hartford.edu/about/campus-leadership/office-provost/faculty-development/ and a listing of Affinity Networks can be found at https://www.hartford.edu/about/diversity-inclusion/affinity-groups.aspx College Information The Hartt School offers accredited undergraduate and graduate programs in music, dance, and theatre. The Hartt School is a school of performing arts within a mid-size university setting. Hartt students benefit from a diverse array of master classes, artist residencies, internships, and partnerships with leading professional performing arts organizations that help them achieve mastery in their discipline, find personal fulfillment, and transition successfully from college to career. The Hartt School has a total enrollment of 460 undergraduate and 120 graduate students served by 43 full-time and 133 part-time faculty with a 12:1 faculty to student ratio. Hartt Dance prepares students for successful, lifelong careers in dance through rigorous and innovative learning experiences. With international and industry opportunities, students gain skills to thrive. Our unique curriculum integrates broad skill sets and supports performers, teachers, and choreographers, giving students exposure to the wider field of dance-related occupations. The Bachelor of Fine Arts in Dance Performance offers extensive performance opportunities, conservatory-style training, and instruction from renowned faculty. Students study ballet, modern, and contemporary dance, along with music, composition, dance teaching, performance techniques, kinesiology, dance technology, and dance history. The BFA in Dance Performance program serves 35 dance majors and prepares students for professional careers in performance. Its alumni are currently performing, or have recently performed, with leading companies including PHILADANCO !, Paul Taylor Dance Company, and the Martha Graham Dance Company, among others. The University of Hartford is fully accredited by the National Association of Schools of Dance. For more information about Hartt Dance, please visit: https://www.hartford.edu/academics/schools-colleges/hartt/academics/dance/default.aspx. Posting Detail Information Posting Number F224P Open Date 08/16/2027 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Special Instructions to Applicants Within the required Statement on Inclusive Excellence and Belonging, candidates should include an explanation of how their teaching practice meets the needs of the diverse population of students at the University of Hartford, focusing particularly on pedagogical approaches that support student success. If relevant, candidates should also comment on research, scholarship, practice, or creative activity that will contribute to the inclusive excellence goals to which the University of Hartford is committed. It is recommended that candidates approach this statement thoughtfully and use specific examples to illustrate their Statement on Inclusive Excellence and Belonging. In your cover letter, please relate your experience to all Qualifications of the position in addition to highlighting what unique skills you would bring to The Hartt School. Priority will be given to applications received by December 18, 2026; however, the search will remain open until the position is filled. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Are you currently or have you ever been a part-time faculty member at the University of Hartford? Yes No Documents Needed To Apply Required Documents Statement on Inclusive Excellence and Belonging Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching Philosophy Optional Documents 200 Bloomfield Avenue\r\nWest Hartford, CT 06117 Back to top Copyright © 2021 University of Hartford To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
